#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b022811-fdd6-56a1-92ee-388e7ea53a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "a66e0264-85ff-5ded-af5d-dc17a9278226" :question/math-academy-id "e-3231" :question/problem "Find the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $y = 0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =4 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept occurs at the point $(-3,0)$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/62/6242b2af154ffea71d523bea97cb44888077b1634f6dd3d2ee05da4feb8e3f5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9faf6d8-0c06-5bf4-bc41-6d68d3d414c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Circle Does Not Intersect an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "905861f1-14b5-59f8-980e-6bd962740620" :question/math-academy-id "e-3232" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the circle $(x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ 9+(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-1)^2 \\geq 0$ for all values of $y,$ there is no $y$ such that $(y-1)^2 = -5.$ Therefore, the circle has no $y$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c8fe8a8d7b0d8946fa5c7e1122df40d16175e0bc48353f53ac30487e3dea85e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Intersection of a Circle With the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "31c41251-e19b-5933-b5f5-6cee31f7f633" :question/math-academy-id "e-3233" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercepts of the circle $x^2+y^2+2x-2y= 8,$ shown below. \n\n![](images/8e/8eb9a6488191e8cf8a5b6107f3501e7d01938cf071e77b75890d570fc99cbf7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We must have $x = 0$ at the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x = 0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =8 \\\\ (0)^{2}+{y}^{2}+2(0)-2y & =8 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =8+1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts occur at  $(0,-2)$ and $(0,4).$"}]}
